  <header class="qa-header">
    <p class="qa-pillar">Question · Biology</p>
    <h1>How Many Eggs Do Ravens Lay?</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens typically lay <strong>3–7 eggs</strong> per clutch, with 4–6 most common in the Common Raven (<em>Corvus corax</em>). Clutch size varies by species, habitat quality, and food availability. Incubation lasts approximately 20–21 days. Some species — notably the Chihuahuan Raven — consistently lay larger clutches (4–7 eggs) than typical large corvids.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Clutch Size by Species</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Clutch size varies across raven species:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup><sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Common Raven (<em>C. corax</em>):</strong> typically 4–6 eggs (range 3–7). One of the more variable large corvids.</li>
      <li><strong>Chihuahuan Raven (<em>C. cryptoleucus</em>):</strong> typically 4–7 eggs — larger average clutch than most raven species, possibly related to the high productivity (but unpredictability) of its desert grassland range.</li>
      <li><strong>Australian Raven (<em>C. coronoides</em>):</strong> typically 4–6 eggs.</li>
      <li><strong>White-necked Raven (<em>C. albicollis</em>):</strong> typically 3–5 eggs.</li>
      <li><strong>Thick-billed Raven (<em>C. crassirostris</em>):</strong> typically 3–5 eggs.</li>
      <li><strong>Brown-necked Raven (<em>C. ruficollis</em>):</strong> typically 3–5 eggs.</li>
      <li><strong>Fan-tailed Raven (<em>C. rhipidurus</em>):</strong> typically 3–5 eggs.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>What Affects Clutch Size?</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Several factors influence how many eggs a raven lays in a given year:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Female condition:</strong> well-nourished females typically lay larger clutches. Food availability in the weeks before egg-laying directly affects clutch size.</li>
      <li><strong>Breeding experience:</strong> first-time breeders typically lay 1–2 fewer eggs than experienced pairs. Clutch size tends to increase with age up to peak reproductive years.</li>
      <li><strong>Habitat quality:</strong> ravens near reliable food sources (e.g., large mammal carcasses, landfill sites, fish spawning runs) tend to produce larger clutches than birds in marginal habitats.</li>
      <li><strong>Latitude:</strong> some studies suggest slightly larger clutches at higher latitudes, possibly reflecting higher seasonal resource pulses.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Incubation and Hatching</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Incubation begins with the first or second egg and lasts approximately 20–21 days in most raven species. Because incubation begins before the clutch is complete, eggs hatch asynchronously — over 1–2 days — producing chicks of slightly different ages and sizes. This asynchronous hatching creates a size hierarchy among nestlings: in poor food years, the youngest (smallest) chick may not survive. In good years, all may fledge.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <p>Chicks fledge at approximately 5–6 weeks old in most species. Post-fledging dependence on parents continues for several weeks to several months depending on species and habitat.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Related Pages</h2>
    <ul>
